<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Basotho Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1822 CE – 1868 CE (approx.) · capital Thaba Bosiu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The mountain kingdom Moshoeshoe built from refugees of the 1820s wars, the forerunner of modern Lesotho.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a>, a junior <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bakwena-chiefdom">Kwena</a> chief, gathered followers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butha-buthe">Butha-Buthe</a> in the early 1820s and in 1824 moved them to the flat-topped mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>, which proved impregnable. As the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Lifaqane</a> scattered communities across the highveld, he welcomed refugees from many groups, lending them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and building a kingdom from them.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a> ruled through his sons and allied chiefs, with public assemblies, pitso, to debate policy. He used diplomacy as much as war, invited missionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Evangelical Missionary Society to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morija">Morija</a> in 1833 and acquired guns and horses. Grain farming in the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon</a> valley fed his people, and he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekonyela">Sekonyela</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/batlokwa-chiefdom">Batlokwa Chiefdom</a> in 1853.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State</a> in 1858 and 1865-1866 cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> much of their lowland farmland. To save the rest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a> sought British protection, and the kingdom was annexed as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basutoland">Basutoland</a> in 1868, two years before his death.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1822 CE – 1868 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.96%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1822 CE – 1868 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thaba Bosiu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moshoeshoe I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sotho traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sesotho</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lesotho, Kingdom of Moshoeshoe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=basotho-kingdom" aria-label="Basotho Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5401 3251 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Basotho Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5721 3507L5716 3505"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5716 3505h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5721 3507h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5721 3507h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Thaba Bosiu. <a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=basotho-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
